{
  // Deliberately EMPTY — decided in build order 18, superseding §8's line about
  // "composite indexes for issues filtered by projectId / cycleId / milestoneId".
  //
  // That line assumed the app would query Firestore per project/cycle/milestone.
  // It does not, and by design: hooks/useEntitySync.ts subscribes to each whole
  // collection with onSnapshot(collection(db, path)) and every by-project,
  // by-cycle, by-milestone and by-filter read is an array pass over the store
  // (useProjectIssues, useCycleIssues, lib/progress.ts, lib/issueFilters.ts).
  // There is not one where() / query() / orderBy() call in src/.
  //
  // Composite indexes exist to serve compound QUERIES. With no query there is
  // nothing to serve, and Firestore already maintains single-field indexes
  // automatically — so adding them now would be config that can only rot: it
  // would drift from the real access pattern and imply a query shape the app
  // does not have.
  //
  // WHEN THIS CHANGES: the hydrate-all model is bounded by workspace size. The
  // day one collection outgrows a full subscription and useEntitySync grows a
  // where()/limit(), the matching composite index belongs here in the SAME
  // change — a missing index surfaces as a runtime FAILED_PRECONDITION with a
  // console link, never as a slow query, so it cannot go unnoticed.
  "indexes": [],
  "fieldOverrides": []
}
